Reasoning: 502s that started with upstream keepalive point at connection reuse: nginx grabs an idle pooled socket the backend already closed. GETs survive because proxy_next_upstream retries them; POSTs are not retried. Read the proxy error.
<|call:tail_error_log|> {"host": "edge-api-1", "grep": "upstream", "lines": 80}
System Response: ~78/10min "upstream prematurely closed connection while reading response header" and "recv() failed (104: Connection reset by peer)", spread evenly across 10.2.0.11/12/13.
Recovery reasoning: Even spread across all three nodes fits a reuse fault, not one sick host. Check the health probe.
<|call:check_upstream_health|> {"upstream": "orders_pool"}
System Response: 3/3 up, active probes 200 OK every 5s, 0 failures in 24h.
Recovery reasoning: Those probes prove nothing: each opens a fresh TCP connection, so none rides an idle pooled socket -- the only kind that fails. Inspect keepalive on both sides.
<|call:grep_nginx_conf|> {"pattern": "keepalive|proxy_http_version", "file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/orders.conf"}
System Response: upstream keepalive 32; proxy_http_version 1.1 and Connection "" set -- reuse wired right. No keepalive_timeout, so nginx holds idle sockets the default 60s; gunicorn runs --keep-alive 5.
Recovery reasoning: Root cause: gunicorn drops idle sockets at 5s while nginx holds them 60s, so reuse after 5s idle hits a dead socket. Cap nginx below the backend.
<|call:patch_nginx_conf|> {"file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/orders.conf", "change": "upstream orders_pool: add keepalive_timeout 4s"}
System Response: nginx -t OK.
<|call:nginx_reload|> {"host": "edge-api-1"}
System Response: reloaded; 502s 160/min -> 0 over 10min, reuse still active.
Resolution: 502s stopped with pooling intact. Root cause: nginx's 60s idle hold outlived gunicorn's 5s keep-alive; keepalive_timeout 4s ends reuse before the backend hangs up. POSTs surfaced it because they are never retried.

Reasoning: nginx dialing an address DNS no longer returns smells like stale resolution, but confirm what the record serves from this box.
<|call:dns_resolve|> {"name": "billing.internal", "server": "10.0.0.2"}
System Response: billing.internal 30 IN A 10.40.2.11, 10.40.2.12 -- authoritative, TTL 30s, no 10.30.1.x.
Recovery reasoning: A clean answer tempts chasing routing, but the error log shows nginx dialing 10.30.1.21, which DNS no longer returns. That mismatch is the tell: a static "server billing.internal" in an upstream block is resolved once at config load and cached until reload. Check the config and load age.
<|call:grep_nginx_conf|> {"pattern": "billing|resolver", "file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/billing.conf"}
System Response: upstream billing { server billing.internal:8443; } -- static hostname, no resolver. Last reload 41 days ago.
Recovery reasoning: Confirmed: workers hold a 41-day-old A record. Reload re-resolves now, then make resolution runtime.
<|call:nginx_reload|> {"host": "edge-bill-1"}
System Response: reloaded; new workers resolved -> 10.40.2.11/12; 502 rate 100% -> 0.4% in 60s.
Recovery reasoning: Restored. Durable fix: move the hostname to a variable with a resolver so nginx honors the 30s TTL instead of pinning at load.
<|call:patch_nginx_conf|> {"file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/billing.conf", "change": "add resolver 10.0.0.2 valid=30s; set $bill_be billing.internal:8443; proxy_pass https://$bill_be"}
System Response: nginx -t OK; reloaded; runtime re-resolution active.
Resolution: /billing serving 200s. Root cause: nginx resolves static upstream hostnames once at config load, so 41-day-old workers pinned the terminated 10.30.1.x IPs despite correct DNS; variable proxy_pass + resolver makes future cutovers take effect within TTL.

Reasoning: An nginx box thrashing its own disk while proxying suggests proxy temp files: buffered responses overflowing memory buffers spill to /var/cache/nginx. Check the disk.
<|call:disk_usage|> {"host": "reports-edge-1", "path": "/var/cache/nginx"}
System Response: proxy_temp: 7.3GB across 41 files sized 60-180MB, heavy create/delete churn.
Recovery reasoning: 41 temp files matches the ~40 concurrent exports -- nginx is staging whole responses on disk. Check the backend dashboard the team cited.
<|call:metrics_query|> {"query": "reporting-api export duration p50/p99", "window": "1h"}
System Response: [OK] p50 0.7s, p99 0.9s, all 200s; a 180MB export leaves the app in ~6s. Verdict: backend healthy.
Recovery reasoning: That green panel is the mechanism, not an alibi: with proxy_buffering on, nginx swallows 180MB in ~6s while a 2MB/s tablet needs ~90s to drain it -- the gap lands in proxy_temp, and 40 x 180MB gives the 7.3GB. Confirm buffering is on defaults.
<|call:grep_nginx_conf|> {"pattern": "proxy_buffer|proxy_max_temp", "file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/reports.conf"}
System Response: no proxy_buffering directive (default on), proxy_buffers 8x8k, no proxy_max_temp_file_size (default 1024m).
Recovery reasoning: For large downloads the right shape is unbuffered proxying: the client's pace backpressures the upstream instead of disk absorbing the gap. 40 concurrent fits the 128-worker pool.
<|call:patch_nginx_conf|> {"file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/reports.conf", "change": "location /reports/export: add proxy_buffering off"}
System Response: nginx -t OK; reloaded; proxy_temp 0.2GB and falling, /var util 12% iowait 2%, export TTFB p99 0.5s.
Resolution: Disk thrash gone, TTFB p99 back to 0.5s. Root cause: default proxy_buffering spilled 180MB responses to proxy_temp for slow clients -- 40 concurrent exports churned ~7GB and starved /var; proxy_buffering off streams at client pace.

Reasoning: Instant 413 with nothing reaching the backend is nginx enforcing client_max_body_size before proxying. Read the effective limit.
<|call:grep_nginx_conf|> {"pattern": "client_max_body_size", "file": "/etc/nginx/sites-enabled/upload-edge.conf"}
System Response: server block: client_max_body_size 200m; (only match in the file)
Recovery reasoning: 200m should admit 100MB, which invites blaming the client -- but nginx applies the most specific context, and an include can hide a location-level override. Reproduce that nginx rejects, then hunt includes.
<|call:curl_with_headers|> {"url": "https://upload-edge-1/v2/attachments", "method": "POST", "body_file": "/tmp/20mb.bin", "headers": {"Content-Type": "application/octet-stream"}}
System Response: HTTP/1.1 413, server nginx, 3ms; attachments-svc log: no entry.
Recovery reasoning: A 20MB body bounces despite the visible 200m, so a lower-level override wins. Search every included fragment.
<|call:grep_nginx_conf|> {"pattern": "client_max_body_size", "file": "/etc/nginx/snippets/api_common.conf"}
System Response: client_max_body_size 8m; -- included by location /v2/ (added last quarter for form endpoints), overriding the server 200m for every /v2/ route.
Recovery reasoning: Root cause: the shared /v2/ snippet caps bodies at 8m, matching ~18% failing (videos) while small photos pass. Raise the limit only for the attachments route.
<|call:patch_nginx_conf|> {"file": "/etc/nginx/sites-enabled/upload-edge.conf", "change": "add location /v2/attachments with client_max_body_size 100m and the same proxy_pass as /v2/"}
System Response: nginx -t OK; reloaded; a 95MB POST returns 201 Created after 41s and the object is stored.
Resolution: 100MB uploads succeed. Root cause: a location-level client_max_body_size 8m in an included /v2/ snippet silently overrode the server-level 200m -- nginx honors the most specific context, so the server value never applied to /v2/.

Reasoning: Zero rejects plus a 2s jump right after adding limit_req suggests requests are queued, not dropped. Split the latency between nginx and upstream.
<|call:metrics_query|> {"query": "p95 request_time vs upstream_response_time route=/search", "window": "6h"}
System Response: upstream_response_time p95 flat 0.095s; request_time p95 2.1s in spikes, gap tops out near 2.0s.
Recovery reasoning: The extra two seconds live inside nginx. If the limiter were engaging the error log should say so.
<|call:tail_error_log|> {"host": "search-edge-1", "grep": "limit_req", "lines": 100}
System Response: 3 lines in 24h "limiting requests, excess: 100.480 by zone api". Otherwise silent. error_log level: error.
Recovery reasoning: The near-silent log makes the limiter look idle, but that's a logging artifact: rejections log at error, delays at warn, and this vhost records only error and above -- hiding the queuing. And burst/rate = 100/50 = the 2.0s gap. Without nodelay, limit_req holds excess in the burst queue and drips it at 50 r/s. Confirm nodelay is absent.
<|call:grep_nginx_conf|> {"pattern": "limit_req", "file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/search.conf"}
System Response: location /search: limit_req zone=api burst=100; -- no nodelay, no delay=.
Recovery reasoning: Add nodelay so burst slots are consumed immediately; a 140 r/s spike then passes at full speed while the zone still enforces the 50 r/s average and rejects beyond burst.
<|call:patch_nginx_conf|> {"file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/search.conf", "change": "location /search: limit_req zone=api burst=100 nodelay"}
System Response: nginx -t OK; reloaded; spike p95 128ms, rejects still 0, bucket drains between spikes.
Resolution: Spike p95 back to ~130ms with protection intact. Root cause: limit_req without nodelay delays burst up to burst/rate = 2.0s; the delays were invisible because they log at warn while the vhost logged only error.

Reasoning: The error log names one hot client, so first ask whether 151.101.64.10 is a genuine abuser to block. Pull the limiter evidence.
<|call:tail_error_log|> {"host": "api-edge-1", "grep": "limiting requests", "lines": 50}
System Response: thousands/min "limiting requests, excess: 20.940 by zone perip, client: 151.101.64.10" plus five more in 151.101.64.0/18 -- reads like a small botnet.
Recovery reasoning: Before treating it as an attack, timing argues otherwise: it began the minute traffic moved behind the CDN, and 151.101.64.0/18 is the CDN's egress range. If nginx sees only POP IPs, the key collapses every user into ~6 buckets of 20 r/s = 120 r/s against 3k r/s of real traffic, 429ing the rest. Compare remote_addr vs X-Forwarded-For.
<|call:tail_access_log|> {"host": "api-edge-1", "fields": ["remote_addr", "http_x_forwarded_for"], "lines": 200}
System Response: 6 distinct remote_addr (all 151.101.64.x) but 187 distinct end-client IPs in X-Forwarded-For.
Recovery reasoning: Misattribution confirmed, not abuse. Fix with realip: trust XFF only from the CDN's ranges so $remote_addr -- and the limiter key -- becomes the end client; real_ip_recursive walks past intermediate hops, and trusting only CDN CIDRs keeps spoofed XFF on direct hits meaningless.
<|call:patch_nginx_conf|> {"file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/api.conf", "change": "add set_real_ip_from 151.101.0.0/16; real_ip_header X-Forwarded-For; real_ip_recursive on"}
System Response: nginx -t OK.
<|call:nginx_reload|> {"host": "api-edge-1"}
System Response: reloaded; access log records true client IPs; 429 rate 40% -> 0.3%, residual maps to clients genuinely over 20 r/s.
Resolution: Per-client limiting restored. Root cause: after the CDN cutover nginx keyed limit_req on POP egress IPs, collapsing all users into ~6 buckets with a 120 r/s aggregate cap against 3k r/s; realip with trusted CDN ranges rekeyed the zone on real client addresses.

Reasoning: 500s under load while the connection gauge sits at 50% means the accounting is wrong. Check nginx's own numbers.
<|call:check_worker_metrics|> {"host": "edge-shop-2", "metrics": ["active", "writing", "waiting"]}
System Response: active 2,048, writing 1,910, waiting 96 -- flat plateau at ~2,050; capacity annotated 4,096 (4 x 1024).
Recovery reasoning: A plateau at exactly half of 4,096 is the tell: stub_status counts client connections, but every proxied request also holds an upstream connection in the same pool. 2,048 clients + 2,048 upstream legs = 4,096 -- already at the ceiling. The error log should confirm.
<|call:tail_error_log|> {"host": "edge-shop-2", "lines": 60}
System Response: flood of "1024 worker_connections are not enough" (~90/s) plus "socket() failed (24: Too many open files) while connecting to upstream".
Recovery reasoning: Two ceilings: worker_connections 1024, and EMFILE means the per-process fd limit sits near 1024 too -- one fd per connection, so raising worker_connections without worker_rlimit_nofile just moves the wall. Verify fd headroom.
<|call:list_open_fds|> {"host": "edge-shop-2", "process": "nginx: worker"}
System Response: workers at 1,019-1,023 fds each; soft limit 1024; no worker_rlimit_nofile.
Recovery reasoning: 4,500 clients needs ~9,000 slots counting upstream legs; 4 x 4096 = 16,384 gives headroom, and the fd limit must exceed the connection cap.
<|call:patch_nginx_conf|> {"file": "/etc/nginx/nginx.conf", "change": "events worker_connections 4096; top-level worker_rlimit_nofile 8192"}
System Response: nginx -t OK; reloaded; workers report fd limit 8192; active climbs past 3,900; 500 rate 90/s -> 0.
Resolution: Full capacity restored before 21:00. Root cause: each proxied request consumes two worker_connections slots (client + upstream), so 4x1024 saturated at ~2,050 clients; worker_connections 4096 + worker_rlimit_nofile 8192 sized both limits.

Reasoning: A 60s cliff through the proxy but not direct is nginx's default proxy_read_timeout firing between upstream reads. Reproduce.
<|call:curl_with_headers|> {"url": "https://stream-edge-1/api/events/live", "flags": ["-N"], "headers": {"Accept": "text/event-stream"}}
System Response: stream opens, events flow, server closes at 60.02s; reconnect dies again at 60s.
Recovery reasoning: Clean 60.0s repro. The team says a 3600s read timeout is set -- verify what the config holds.
<|call:grep_nginx_conf|> {"pattern": "proxy_read_timeout|location", "file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/stream-edge.conf"}
System Response: location /events/ { proxy_read_timeout 3600s; proxy_buffering off; proxy_http_version 1.1; proxy_pass http://events_svc; } and location /api/ { proxy_pass http://events_svc; }
Recovery reasoning: The config looks fixed, which is why this sat broken a month. But clients hit /api/events/live; nginx matches by longest prefix, so that lands in /api/, not /events/ -- the tuned block serves zero SSE and /api/ runs the 60s default. Confirm which request times out.
<|call:tail_error_log|> {"host": "stream-edge-1", "grep": "timed out", "lines": 40}
System Response: "upstream timed out (110) while reading upstream, request: GET /api/events/live" -- ~18/s.
Recovery reasoning: Confirmed. Give the real path its own location with the SSE trio: long read timeout, buffering off, HTTP/1.1, cleared Connection.
<|call:patch_nginx_conf|> {"file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/stream-edge.conf", "change": "add location /api/events/ with proxy_read_timeout 3600s, proxy_buffering off, proxy_http_version 1.1, Connection cleared, proxy_pass to events_svc"}
System Response: nginx -t OK; reloaded; stream held 1,800s+ with 15s heartbeats; reconnects 1,100/min -> 12/min.
Resolution: Streams stable. Root cause: location precedence -- the 3600s proxy_read_timeout sat in /events/ while clients hit /api/events/live, which matches /api/ and ran the 60s default; a dedicated location fixed it.

Reasoning: gzip working for HTML but not the API path means the gzip filter declines those responses -- type, size, or proxied-request rules. Break egress down by type.
<|call:metrics_query|> {"query": "origin egress by content_type and content_encoding", "window": "24h"}
System Response: text/html 92% gzip-encoded; application/json 0.02% gzip-encoded and 1.4TB/day of the 1.9TB.
Recovery reasoning: Uncompressed JSON is the bulk. Verify from outside what the origin returns for the catalog route.
<|call:curl_with_headers|> {"url": "https://origin-web-1/api/catalog", "headers": {"Accept-Encoding": "gzip"}}
System Response: HTTP/1.1 200, Content-Encoding: gzip, Content-Length: 96412 -- compresses fine from this shell.
Recovery reasoning: A clean gzip to my curl while the CDN gets identity bodies means the requests differ, and gzip_types is fine since my JSON compressed. The tell: the CDN adds a Via header, my curl doesn't, and nginx treats any request with Via as proxied, gated by gzip_proxied (default off). Reproduce as the CDN.
<|call:curl_with_headers|> {"url": "https://origin-web-1/api/catalog", "headers": {"Accept-Encoding": "gzip", "Via": "1.1 cdn-pop-fra2"}}
System Response: HTTP/1.1 200, no Content-Encoding, Content-Length: 1248306 -- identity body for the Via-bearing request.
Recovery reasoning: Confirmed: gzip_proxied off skips exactly the CDN traffic. Enable it for proxied requests and add gzip_vary.
<|call:patch_nginx_conf|> {"file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/origin-web.conf", "change": "http block: add gzip_proxied any and gzip_vary on"}
System Response: nginx -t OK; reloaded; the Via-bearing /api/catalog now returns Content-Encoding: gzip, Vary: Accept-Encoding, Content-Length 96412 -- ~92% smaller.
Resolution: CDN-forwarded /api/catalog ships 96KB instead of 1.2MB. Root cause: gzip_proxied defaults to off and nginx classifies any request bearing Via as proxied, so all CDN traffic skipped compression while header-free curl tests kept confirming gzip as working.

Reasoning: Language mixups behind a proxy cache mean variants collide under one key. nginx stores per-Vary entries, so Vary is stripped or ignored. Reproduce.
<|call:curl_with_headers|> {"url": "https://cdn-origin-1/assets/i18n/app.json", "headers": {"Accept-Language": "de"}}
System Response: HTTP/1.1 200, Content-Language: de, X-Cache-Status: MISS -- German, correct.
Recovery reasoning: Correct German on a MISS proves nothing: a miss hits the backend, which always answers right; the bug only shows on a HIT, which that MISS just seeded -- now request English.
<|call:curl_with_headers|> {"url": "https://cdn-origin-1/assets/i18n/app.json", "headers": {"Accept-Language": "en"}}
System Response: HTTP/1.1 200, Content-Language: de, X-Cache-Status: HIT -- English request got the German body.
Recovery reasoning: Reproduced: one stored variant serves all languages -- nginx honors Vary unless told not to. Check proxy_ignore_headers.
<|call:grep_nginx_conf|> {"pattern": "proxy_ignore_headers|proxy_cache_key", "file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/cdn-origin.conf"}
System Response: proxy_ignore_headers Cache-Control Expires Vary; cache key is $request_uri only -- Vary was added to force-cache the backend's uncacheable responses.
Recovery reasoning: Root cause: ignoring Vary collapses all languages onto one key, first fetch owns it. Keep ignoring Cache-Control/Expires but honor Vary, then flush.
<|call:patch_nginx_conf|> {"file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/cdn-origin.conf", "change": "proxy_ignore_headers Cache-Control Expires (Vary removed)"}
System Response: nginx -t OK; reloaded.
<|call:cache_purge|> {"zone": "i18n_cache", "match": "/assets/i18n/*"}
System Response: 412 entries purged; repopulation stores separate de/en/fr variants; HIT now matches Accept-Language.
Resolution: Cache hits serve the requested language. Root cause: Vary in proxy_ignore_headers made nginx ignore the backend's Vary and keep one variant per URL; dropping it plus a purge restored per-language variants at 93% hit.

Reasoning: 'Unexpected token <' means the browser parsed HTML where it expected JavaScript. With 200s logged nginx thinks it served valid content -- check what those 200s returned.
<|call:tail_access_log|> {"host": "spa-edge-1", "filter": "chunk-7f3a91.js", "fields": ["status", "body_bytes_sent"], "lines": 20}
System Response: 20/20 lines status=200, body_bytes_sent=684 for every request.
Recovery reasoning: The uniform 200s read healthy, but 684 bytes for a code-split chunk that should be tens of KB gives it away -- an HTML shell. Fetch it.
<|call:curl_with_headers|> {"url": "https://spa-edge-1/static/js/chunk-7f3a91.js", "headers": {}}
System Response: HTTP/1.1 200, Content-Type: text/html, 684 bytes, body starts '<!doctype html>' -- it is index.html.
Recovery reasoning: nginx serves the SPA shell for a missing file: the deploy replaced the hashed chunks, so chunk-7f3a91.js is gone and it falls through try_files into /index.html. Confirm the config.
<|call:grep_nginx_conf|> {"pattern": "try_files|location", "file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/spa.conf"}
System Response: location / { root /srv/spa/current; try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html; } -- no separate /static/ location, so missing assets fall back to index.html at 200.
Recovery reasoning: The fallback is right for app routes, wrong for assets: a missing chunk must 404 so the app's chunk-load handler reloads onto fresh HTML instead of running HTML as JS. Give assets their own location.
<|call:patch_nginx_conf|> {"file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/spa.conf", "change": "add location /static/ with try_files $uri =404 and immutable Cache-Control"}
System Response: nginx -t OK; reloaded; chunk-7f3a91.js now 404s, current chunk-c2e410.js serves 200 application/javascript; Sentry 700/min -> 8/min.
Resolution: Stale chunk requests 404 and the SPA reloads onto the new build. Root cause: the catch-all try_files ... /index.html also covered /static/, so chunks deleted by the deploy were served index.html at 200 and run as JavaScript.

Reasoning: A handshake alert from the upstream right after an SNI-routing layer appeared points at the ClientHello -- what nginx presents no longer satisfies the ingress. Quantify.
<|call:tail_error_log|> {"host": "edge-pay-2", "grep": "SSL_do_handshake", "lines": 30}
System Response: ~45/s "SSL_do_handshake() failed (sslv3 alert handshake failure) while SSL handshaking to upstream, upstream: 10.44.0.20:8443" -- both members.
Recovery reasoning: Deterministic on both. Reproduce the team's passing check from this box to rule out network/trust-store.
<|call:openssl_s_client|> {"host": "10.44.0.20", "port": 8443, "servername": "payments.internal"}
System Response: handshake OK: TLSv1.3, cert CN=payments.internal, verify return 0 -- TLS looks healthy.
Recovery reasoning: That success is the misleading proof: s_client -servername puts SNI in the ClientHello, but nginx's proxy sends no SNI by default (proxy_ssl_server_name off) -- an SNI router with no name to route on answers with an alert. Probe the way nginx connects: no server name.
<|call:openssl_s_client|> {"host": "10.44.0.20", "port": 8443, "servername": null}
System Response: alert handshake failure right after ClientHello, no certificate -- identical to nginx's error.
Recovery reasoning: Proven: the ingress requires SNI, nginx sends none. Enable proxy_ssl_server_name and pin proxy_ssl_name, else proxy_pass to an upstream block derives the name from the block label.
<|call:patch_nginx_conf|> {"file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/payments.conf", "change": "location /pay/: add proxy_ssl_server_name on and proxy_ssl_name payments.internal"}
System Response: nginx -t OK; reloaded; /pay/health returns 200 via 10.44.0.20:8443, error log clean 5min, 502 rate 100% -> 0%.
Resolution: Payments path restored. Root cause: nginx omitted SNI upstream -- proxy_ssl_server_name defaults to off and the SNI-routing ingress rejects a nameless ClientHello; the team's proof passed only because -servername supplied the SNI nginx omitted.

Reasoning: Slow-but-successful checkouts with one crashlooping member fit retry latency: nginx wastes an attempt on the sick server, then retries elsewhere while the user eats the dead attempt. Look for retries.
<|call:tail_error_log|> {"host": "orders-edge-1", "grep": "upstream", "lines": 60}
System Response: bursts every ~10s "connect() failed (111: Connection refused), upstream: 10.2.0.13:8080" (app-3).
Recovery reasoning: Bursts spaced at fail_timeout mean app-3 re-enters rotation: nginx passive checks readmit it 10s after a down-mark, so three more requests must fail to eject it. Confirm the pod is really sick first.
<|call:upstream_app_logs|> {"service": "orders-app-3", "lines": 40}
System Response: /healthz 200 OK continuously at 12ms, green across the window; Kubernetes marks the container Ready.
Recovery reasoning: A green /healthz on a pod that refuses real connections is the contradiction, not proof: the probe hits a trivial handler while the OOM-looping worker pool can't accept traffic. Trust the data path.
<|call:tail_access_log|> {"host": "orders-edge-1", "filter": "path=/checkout", "fields": ["upstream_addr", "upstream_response_time"], "lines": 20}
System Response: slow lines: upstream_addr "10.2.0.13, 10.2.0.14", upstream_response_time "3.98, 0.22" -- a ~4s dead attempt then success; 3.98+0.22 = the 4.2s p99, and 1-in-4 hitting app-3 first = the 25%.
Recovery reasoning: Pull app-3 until its OOM is fixed and cheapen failed attempts.
<|call:patch_nginx_conf|> {"file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/orders.conf", "change": "upstream orders_pool: mark 10.2.0.13:8080 down; add proxy_connect_timeout 2s"}
System Response: nginx -t OK; reloaded; checkout p99 back to 300ms in 3min; retry bursts gone.
Resolution: Latency recovered. Root cause: passive checks readmitted crashlooping app-3 every fail_timeout=10s while /healthz stayed green (it never exercises the dead worker pool), so 25% of checkouts paid a ~4s failed attempt before retry; marking it down fixed it.

Reasoning: A cluster-wide hit-ratio cliff at the minute a server left suggests the routing function changed, not the caches. Confirm the correlation.
<|call:metrics_query|> {"query": "cache_hit_ratio per node, db_qps", "window": "3h"}
System Response: all 5 remaining nodes step ~88% -> ~64% at 09:20 sharp; db_qps 4.8k -> 14.4k same minute; no node restarted.
Recovery reasoning: A uniform drop on every node rules out one cold instance. Check how the upstream hashes.
<|call:grep_nginx_conf|> {"pattern": "upstream search|hash", "file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/search.conf"}
System Response: upstream search_pool { hash $arg_tenant; server search-1,2,3,5,6.internal:9200; } -- no 'consistent' parameter; search-4 deleted at 09:20.
Recovery reasoning: Plain hash maps keys modulo the live count -- but verify the caches didn't just evict cold, which needs a different fix.
<|call:cache_inspect|> {"node": "search-2.internal", "stats": ["fill", "evictions"]}
System Response: cache 91% full, near-zero evictions, top tenants unchanged since 08:00 -- data warm and intact.
Recovery reasoning: The warm, full cache is the deceptive reading: residency is fine, routing is not. Modulo hashing over 6 -> 5 rehashes ~83% of tenants to a node that lacks their warm entries. Fix: re-add search-4 marked down -- in the hash set but taking no traffic, restoring the 6-slot mapping; then move to consistent hashing so future changes remap only ~1/N.
<|call:patch_nginx_conf|> {"file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/search.conf", "change": "upstream search_pool: re-add server search-4.internal:9200 down; consistent-hash migration scheduled for 03:00"}
System Response: nginx -t OK; reloaded; mapping restored; hit ratio 64% -> 85% in 12min, db_qps 14.4k -> 6.0k and falling.
Resolution: Hit ratio 64% -> 85% and DB off the ceiling. Root cause: a non-consistent hash upstream rehashed ~83% of tenants off their warm caches on removal; re-adding search-4 down restored the mapping, consistent hashing planned next.
